WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:07.251
Bodily harm, betrayal, braggadocio. Would it even&nbsp;
be an Iron Man movie without a few particulars?

00:00:07.251 --> 00:00:10.440
"From the ashes of captivity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:15.000
never has a greater phoenix metaphor&nbsp;
been personified in human history."

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:20.220
Anyone even vaguely familiar with Tony&nbsp;
Stark knows the character suffers from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:24.120
a massive superiority complex.&nbsp;
Tony is a world-changing genius,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:28.440
and he is highly aware of that fact. This&nbsp;
leads Tony to believe that he knows better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:32.640
than anyone else, to the frustration of his&nbsp;
allies and the advantage of his enemies.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.240
That's why a major plot point&nbsp;
in each "Iron Man" movie is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:41.100
Tony learning humility the hard way. Tony&nbsp;
begins the first film as an arrogant and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:45.600
selfish weapons manufacturer. It's only&nbsp;
after he is captured by terrorists that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.560
he sees the destruction caused by his&nbsp;
weapons, and vows to stop making them.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:54.960
In the second movie, Tony has trouble finding a&nbsp;
solution to his deadly illness. Tony undergoes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.940
a reality check, realizing that even his great&nbsp;
intelligence is not enough to rescue him from his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:05.280
condition. Instead, he has to rely on his father's&nbsp;
help from beyond the grave to find a solution.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.940
In "Iron Man 3", Tony's&nbsp;
trademark arrogance crumbles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:13.500
when a mysterious terrorist called the&nbsp;
Mandarin attacks him in his own home in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:18.420
broad daylight. Tony is forced to concede&nbsp;
he cannot handle this new threat alone and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:23.820
needs the help of his friends to defeat the&nbsp;
villain, all while keeping a grip on his PTSD.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:29.460
Female love interests in the MCU too often get&nbsp;
the short end of the stick, but Tony's longtime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:34.620
love interest, Pepper Potts, bucks this trend by&nbsp;
being an important part of the "Iron Man" films.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:38.820
Pepper starts out as Tony's assistant,&nbsp;
and the closest thing to a companion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:43.380
he has in his jet-setting, playboy life.&nbsp;
Throughout the movie, Pepper supports Tony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:48.180
even saving his life at one point. In the&nbsp;
second film, Tony and Pepper are officially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:52.860
a couple. Dealing with his personal illness,&nbsp;
Tony makes Pepper the head of Stark Industries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.340
and she takes over the entire&nbsp;
organization on Tony's behalf.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:01.140
But it's in "Iron Man 3" when Tony needs&nbsp;
Pepper the most. Struggling to control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:06.840
his PTSD following the Battle of New York, Tony&nbsp;
leans hard on Pepper to keep him going through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:12.120
his insomnia and night terrors. In the end,&nbsp;
Pepper once again personally saves Tony's life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:17.700
when she also briefly gains superpowers&nbsp;
and helps Tony defeat Aldrich Killian.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:23.880
When "Iron Man" premiered back in 2008, one major&nbsp;
reason why Tony Stark felt like a breath of fresh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:29.040
air is that his characterization was so different&nbsp;
from the popular superhero archetypes of the day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:34.440
Heroes like Spider-Man and Batman had tortured&nbsp;
inner lives with an unhealthy amount of angst.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:37.260
Tony is refreshingly free of such a mentality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:41.040
and does not usually suffer from a&nbsp;
great deal of self-doubt. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.540
Tony is so convinced of his own personal&nbsp;
greatness that he routinely makes a point of it.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:50.281
No moment exemplifies this better than&nbsp;
the end of the first "Iron Man" film.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:51.600
"I am Iron Man."

00:02:51.600 --> 00:03:01.090
In the next film, Tony frames his ownership&nbsp;
of the Iron Man suit as a win for America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:03.420
informing a court convened against him:

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:10.657
"I've successfully privatized world&nbsp;
peace. What more do you want?"

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:15.780
Tony Stark is many things. He's a&nbsp;
playboy, a billionaire, a philanthropist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:21.420
and a superhero. But before all of that,&nbsp;
Tony is, at his core, an engineer and an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:26.100
inventor. The entire Iron Man saga begins&nbsp;
because Tony is captured by terrorists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:30.540
and the only way to escape is to create a suit&nbsp;
of armor that could get him out of the situation.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:34.080
The first Iron Man suit was&nbsp;
built in a cave from spare parts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:39.480
and it's a very rough affair: big, bulky and&nbsp;
not very fuel-efficient. After returning home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:42.780
Tony gets to work creating a&nbsp;
better-designed version of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:47.220
suit — which eventually becomes the iconic&nbsp;
red-and-gold armor we all fell in love with.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:50.760
With each new movie after that,&nbsp;
including the "Avengers" flicks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:55.800
Tony debuts new versions of the classic Iron&nbsp;
Man suit. There's the emergency suit hidden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:00.360
in a briefcase from "Iron Man 2." There's&nbsp;
the army of autonomous suits from "Iron Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:06.240
3." There's the Hulkbuster armor from "Avengers:&nbsp;
Age of Ultron" and "Infinity War." And finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:10.709
the nanotech suit that Tony wears in&nbsp;
his battle against Thanos and his army.

00:04:10.865 --> 00:04:16.200
The "Iron Man" films are considered the starting&nbsp;
point for the MCU. Not just because they were the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:20.040
first movies in the franchise to receive&nbsp;
critical acclaim and box office success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:25.740
but also because many of the plot points from the&nbsp;
"Iron Man" movies became a staple of the MCU as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:31.020
a whole. For instance, Tony's quippy nature&nbsp;
has come to be shared by many other heroes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:36.180
as has the "the villain has the same power set&nbsp;
as the hero" thing started by Obadiah Stane.

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:41.040
The villain also tends to be someone whom the&nbsp;
hero has scorned in the past. In "Iron Man,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:46.620
Tony is so busy working on dismantling his past&nbsp;
as a weapons manufacturer, that he doesn't realize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:51.360
that his actions are negatively affecting his&nbsp;
company's second-in-command. This leads to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:55.920
Number 2 stepping up his efforts to usurp Tony&nbsp;
from the company, attempting to kill Tony in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:01.560
the process. In "Iron Man 2," Justin Hammer was&nbsp;
a weapons manufacturer who had spent a lifetime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:06.360
being bested by Tony in their field of work —&nbsp;
a rival looking for an opportunity to one-up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:12.480
Tony by stealing and improving upon his Iron Man&nbsp;
armor design. To this end, he recruits Ivan Vanko,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:17.940
aka Whiplash, whose family was previously&nbsp;
crossed by Stark's. Finally, in "Iron Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:23.400
3," Aldrich Killian was a researcher whom Tony&nbsp;
had once dismissed and embarrassed. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:28.822
Killian builds his own company and dreams&nbsp;
of the day he can finally defeat Tony Stark.

00:05:29.071 --> 00:05:35.100
If Pepper Potts is the voice of reason in Tony&nbsp;
Stark's life, and JARVIS is the blindly faithful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:40.740
ally who will do anything he asks, James "Rhodey"&nbsp;
Rhodes is the happy medium in Tony's inner circle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:45.780
Rhodey was Tony's friend long before he became&nbsp;
Iron Man, and he feels a personal obligation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:51.111
to look out for his reckless comrade — even if it&nbsp;
means breaking the rules or angering Tony himself.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:59.252
"I'm only gonna say this once. Get out."

00:05:59.445 --> 00:06:04.620
As a high-ranking member of the United States Air&nbsp;
Force, Rhodey is often torn between his duty and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:09.120
his friendship with Tony. In the first "Iron&nbsp;
Man," Rhodey is forced to hide the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:14.280
it is Tony inside the Iron Man armor getting&nbsp;
into an impromptu dogfight against a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:19.860
of F-22 Raptors. In "Iron Man 2," irked&nbsp;
by Tony's increasingly unstable behavior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:24.720
Rhodey is forced to go against his wishes and&nbsp;
confiscate a prototype of the Iron Man armor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:27.240
turning it over to the US&nbsp;
Air Force behind his back.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:32.460
Then in "Iron Man 3," Rhodey has become a&nbsp;
new superhero named Iron Patriot. Once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:36.300
Rhodey is forced to work outside the&nbsp;
law when his suit gets controlled by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:40.680
the villain to assassinate the president,&nbsp;
as ordered by Alrich Killian. This time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:44.485
Rhodey and Tony both work in secret&nbsp;
to bring down the villain's plan.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:50.160
The world of comic books is roughly divided&nbsp;
into two. Some heroes believe in working within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:54.840
the law, others believe in working outside of&nbsp;
it. But in the case of Tony Stark, you have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:59.820
little bit of both. While Tony's chief priority&nbsp;
is making the world a better, safer place, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:04.920
has no interest in doing so by working alongside&nbsp;
the government, at least in the "Iron Man" films.

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:09.840
In the first movie, Tony abruptly shuts down&nbsp;
his weapons manufacturing company that had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:14.340
been supplying the government with military&nbsp;
arms for decades. He also tries to keep his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:18.840
identity as Iron Man a secret before once&nbsp;
again abruptly letting the cat out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:24.540
bag. Then "Iron Man 2" deals with the fallout of&nbsp;
Tony's dramatic declaration, as the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:30.840
tries to force him to share the tech behind his&nbsp;
armor. Finally, in Iron Man 3, an inside job to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:35.460
assassinate the President forces Tony and Rhodey&nbsp;
to fly under the radar for the greater good.

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:40.380
Ironically, Tony works with the government&nbsp;
much better in the "Avengers" movies, joining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:45.420
SHIELD despite his suspicions of their motives.&nbsp;
After causing the events of "Avengers: Age of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:50.322
Ultron," Tony signs the Sokovia
Accords as a means of atoning for his actions.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:55.500
When Tony Stark was being cast, the studio&nbsp;
was resistant to the idea of Robert Downey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.500 --> 00:08:00.600
Jr. playing the role. Director Jon Favreau&nbsp;
fought to cast Downey since he felt that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:05.220
actor's very public struggles with his personal&nbsp;
demons would translate very well to Tony Stark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:08.520
a character who has historically&nbsp;
struggled with demons of his own.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:13.680
And so each "Iron Man" movie has an important&nbsp;
side-plot, wherein Tony must learn to confront&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:18.180
his mistakes. In the first movie, Tony finds&nbsp;
out the hard way that the weapons his company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:22.740
manufacture often wind up in the hands&nbsp;
of terrorists. This prompts him to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:28.020
Iron Man and end the destruction his company&nbsp;
had indirectly caused. In the second movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:32.100
Tony's habit of not playing well with&nbsp;
others comes back to bite him, when his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:36.720
incompetent rival works with the US government&nbsp;
to create new versions of the Iron Man armor.

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:42.720
Finally in "Iron Man 3," Tony's refusal to get&nbsp;
help for his PTSD and his distancing himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:47.940
from those seeking to help him lead to severe&nbsp;
and debilitating breakdowns. Tony also gets his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:52.680
butt handed to him by the villain when he tries to&nbsp;
deal with "the Mandarin" on his own. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:58.489
Tony only manages to win after working with&nbsp;
Rhodey and Pepper to bring down Aldrich Killian.

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:04.560
On his own, Tony Stark can be a pretty impossible&nbsp;
character to root for. A self-described "Genius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:09.300
billionaire playboy philanthropist," Tony&nbsp;
combines extreme intelligence with extreme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:14.520
arrogance and snark. He can be hard for general&nbsp;
audiences to like, let alone actively support,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:19.087
especially if we don't get to see Tony in&nbsp;
a vulnerable position from time to time.

00:09:19.087 --> 00:09:25.560
"Bit odd… if this was your last birthday party you&nbsp;
were ever gonna have, how would you celebrate it?"

00:09:25.906 --> 00:09:30.300
To offset his many, many advantages&nbsp;
in life, every "Iron Man" movie has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:34.980
a major plot point about Tony struggling with a&nbsp;
life-threatening condition. In the first movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:39.600
Tony gets severely injured in an explosion,&nbsp;
and the only way to keep him alive is through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:44.940
the miniaturized arc reactor that he invents&nbsp;
and places in his chest. In "Iron Man 2," it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:50.280
is revealed that the arc reactor has started to&nbsp;
poison his entire body. Once again Tony races&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:55.080
against time to find a cure for his condition&nbsp;
before the poison can shut down his body. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.080 --> 00:10:00.240
the final film, Tony suffers from PTSD, which&nbsp;
leaves him a quivering mess and leads him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:05.520
make mistakes that almost get him killed. In&nbsp;
this way, each "Iron Man" movie combines Tony's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:10.409
external struggle against a physical villain&nbsp;
with an internal struggle against his mortality.

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:16.680
While Tony Stark is a very, very smart man,&nbsp;
something he will personally tell you if you let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:20.940
him, his intelligence does not always translate&nbsp;
to a complete awareness of his surroundings.&nbsp;

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:26.040
This leads to a recurring problem, where Tony does&nbsp;
not realize there is a mole in his trusted circle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:30.900
working against his interests. In the first&nbsp;
"Iron Man," the mole is Obadiah Stane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:35.940
who is secretly working to steal Tony's inventions&nbsp;
and eventually kill him off. In the second movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:41.580
SHIELD sends Natasha Romanoff, aka Black&nbsp;
Widow, undercover inside Tony's company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:46.500
to assess his personality, as a prelude to&nbsp;
asking Tony to join the Avengers Initiative.

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:52.200
In "Iron Man 3," Tony has to deal with Maya&nbsp;
Hansen, a former love interest who comes back into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:58.020
his life with details about a radical new medical&nbsp;
procedure called Extremis. Unbeknownst to Tony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.020 --> 00:11:02.700
Maya is secretly working with Aldrich&nbsp;
Killian to turn Extremis into a weapon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:06.960
While Maya comes to regret her association&nbsp;
with Killian and tries to help Tony escape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:10.985
her actions lead to a great deal of&nbsp;
harm being inflicted upon the world.

00:11:11.340 --> 00:11:15.000
Superhero movies generally tend to&nbsp;
keep the villains in the same country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:18.540
as the hero. Guys like Batman&nbsp;
and Superman fight Americans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:22.860
or they fight aliens. But the "Iron&nbsp;
Man" trilogy specifically deals with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:27.000
foreign terrorists, reflecting on Tony&nbsp;
Stark's past as a weapons manufacturer.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:31.260
In the first movie, Tony is captured by a&nbsp;
terrorist group in war-torn Afghanistan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:35.700
The group makes a return later in the movie,&nbsp;
when they are shown to be working alongside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.700 --> 00:11:41.760
Obadiah Stane. In the second movie, we get Russian&nbsp;
criminal and international terrorist Ivan Vanko,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:47.340
aka Whiplash, who nurses a personal grudge&nbsp;
against Tony. Vanko builds his own robot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:52.080
terrorist army after hijacking the knockoff&nbsp;
Iron Man suits built by Justin Hammer.

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:56.940
Then in "Iron Man 3," Tony has to deal with his&nbsp;
greatest enemy from the comics, the Mandarin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.940 --> 00:12:01.680
The film's version of the character is depicted&nbsp;
as the head of a massive international criminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:06.780
organization called "The Ten Rings," who have no&nbsp;
problem carrying out attacks on American soil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.780 --> 00:12:12.180
Tony finds out this terrorist leader version of&nbsp;
The Mandarin was just an actor set up by Killian.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:17.065
This version of the Mandarin was later retconned&nbsp;
in "Shang-Chi and the Legend of the Ten Rings."

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:21.300
Post-credits scenes have become&nbsp;
an expectation for every Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:25.740
movie. But when the original Iron Man&nbsp;
debuted, the concept of a post-credits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:30.240
scene was pretty groundbreaking. These&nbsp;
punctuative teasers became staples of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:34.680
the Marvel Cinematic Universe, hinting at&nbsp;
the next expansion or returning character.

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:40.260
"Iron Man" has probably the most iconic&nbsp;
post-credits scene of all time in the MCU.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:44.580
After many months of speculation, fans&nbsp;
cheered at the end of "Iron Man," when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:48.660
Samuel L. Jackson steps out of the&nbsp;
shadows as Nick Fury and tells Tony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:54.240
Stark about the Avengers Initiative. Iron&nbsp;
Man 2's post-credits scene reveals Thor's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:58.200
hammer sticking out of a crater, signaling&nbsp;
the near-arrival of the God of Thunder.

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:01.260
"Iron Man 3" takes a more easygoing approach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:05.130
with Tony Stark having an impromptu&nbsp;
therapy session with Bruce Banner.

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:06.693
"Are you actively napping?"

00:13:06.693 --> 00:13:09.180
"I was— I, I, I drifted."

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:10.440
"Where did I lose you?"

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:15.480
After three movies' worth of drama, we'd say Tony&nbsp;
earned the right to take it easy this time around.

